<main class="page_shell">
<header class="article_header">
<p class="eyebrow">SURVEILLANCE TECHNOLOGY · VEHICLES</p>
<h1>Automated License Plate Readers</h1>
<p class="article_deck">ALPR systems turn vehicle observations into searchable records. Illinois records document statewide and local deployments, multiple vendors, statutory restrictions, agency-specific retention practices, and data sharing.</p>
</header>

<section class="evidence_box"><span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED IN ILLINOIS · REVIEWED 2026-09-18</span><p>Illinois law expressly defines ALPR devices, ALPR information, and multi-agency/vendor ALPR systems. Illinois State Police maintains a public ALPR transparency page with annual reports, statistics, agreements, directives, and its Motorola Solutions contract.</p></section>

<section class="evidence_box evidence_unknown"><span class="status_label">RETENTION CORRECTION</span><h2>Illinois does not impose one universal 30-day ALPR retention period.</h2><p>625 ILCS 5/2-130 regulates ALPR information and prohibited uses/sharing, but the current statutory text does not create a blanket 30-day deletion rule for every Illinois ALPR system. Thirty-day, seven-day, or other periods shown on NoRec are agency/vendor policies or contract settings only when the cited record says so.</p></section>

<section>
<p class="eyebrow">WHAT THE SYSTEM RECORDS</p><h2>More than a plate number.</h2>
<p>Illinois law defines ALPR broadly around the automated capture and comparison of vehicle or plate information. Commercial systems may add vehicle characteristics, timestamps, camera location, hot-list alerts, and other searchable attributes. Agency-specific capabilities are listed only when supported by records.</p>
</section>

<section>
<p class="eyebrow">ILLINOIS DEPLOYMENTS</p><h2>Statewide and local systems coexist.</h2>
<div class="finding_grid">
<article><span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED · CURRENT</span><h3>Illinois State Police</h3><p>ISP identifies Motorola Solutions as the primary vendor for its statewide ALPR project. Its current transparency page reports 808 state cameras installed statewide and says ISP owns the statewide system hardware and data. February 2026 funding added installations in Bureau, Champaign, Grundy, Henry, Kendall, LaSalle, Madison, Rock Island, and Winnebago counties.</p></article>
<article><span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span><h3>Boone County / Belvidere</h3><p>Local records document Flock Safety ALPR use, Policy 450, retention rules, audits, and Belvidere camera locations.</p></article>
<article><span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span><h3>Huntley</h3><p>Huntley's public-board process documented a 17-camera Flock deployment and vehicle-attribute searching.</p></article>
<article><span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span><h3>Winnebago County / Rockford</h3><p>County and city records document separate Flock deployments, including a five-year Sheriff's agreement and a 2026 Rockford Police contract covering Flock software, LPR, and PTZ systems.</p></article>
<article><span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span><h3>DeKalb</h3><p>DeKalb purchased 12 Genetec readers in 2021 and approved a two-year Flock ALPR service contract in 2026. A separate Axon Fleet 3 agreement also contains mobile ALPR licensing.</p></article>
<article><span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED · CURRENT CONTRACT</span><h3>Kane County</h3><p>Resolution 26-069, passed February 10, 2026, renewed 25 Sheriff Flock LPR cameras countywide at $72,000 per year. Kane County separately approved a Sheriff/Aurora Drone as First Responder trial in August 2026 involving a leased Flock drone platform.</p></article>
</div>
</section>

<section>
<p class="eyebrow">GOVERNANCE</p><h2>Retention, sharing, access, and enforcement</h2>
<div class="provider_questions">
<details open><summary>Retention</summary><p>How long a vendor hosts detections can differ from how long an agency retains downloaded/exported evidence. NoRec records both when the documents allow it.</p></details>
<details><summary>Ownership and sharing</summary><p>Illinois law recognizes multi-agency and vendor ALPR systems. A reader's practical reach can therefore exceed the cameras physically owned by one department. NoRec also keeps state-owned ISP cameras separate from county and municipal systems even when the hardware is physically located inside the same county.</p></details>
<details><summary>Queries and audits</summary><p>Policies may require a reason for searches, individual credentials, audit trails, supervisory review, or periodic audits. NoRec treats the existence of an audit requirement separately from evidence that an audit occurred and what it found.</p></details>
<details><summary>Enforcement</summary><p>An automated alert and the legal basis for enforcement are separate questions. Relevant agency policy and Illinois law govern verification and subsequent action.</p></details>
</div>
</section>

<section>
<p class="eyebrow">STATE PROGRAM STATUS · 2026-09-18</p><h2>State hardware is not a local-agency inventory.</h2>
<p>ISP says it manages procurement, installation, ownership, and maintenance for the statewide Motorola ALPR system, with IDOT providing funding and procurement assistance. NoRec therefore labels those installations as Illinois State Police infrastructure rather than attributing them to whichever county or municipality contains the pole.</p>
</section>

